# Packaging the Loom CLI

The `@athrio/loom` package ships to npm as a single self-contained file. Vite bundles the CLI program, the language server, and Effect into `dist/main.js` and adds a Node shebang, so the file runs directly under Node and, since Bun is Node-compatible, under Bun as well. The published package is therefore pure JavaScript with no runtime dependencies — every library it needs is already inside the bundle. Any Node package manager installs it — npm, pnpm, yarn, or Bun.

## The manifest {Tangle}[package.json]

The `files` field restricts the published package to the `dist` directory, and the `bin` field maps the `loom` command to the bundle. The bundle inlines every dependency, so all dependencies belong in the `devDependencies` field. Loom Design is not among them. The two products carry different licences. The Quadrivium Academy foundation licenses this package under MIT, and Athrio Media OÜ licenses Design under terms of its own, so `loom design` runs Design's binary and this package neither imports nor builds it.

The `typecheck` script reads the source without writing anything — `--noEmit`, because the shared configuration sets an output directory that a check has no use for, and compiled copies of the tests left there are found twice by the test runner. It is the script this package went without, and the cost showed. Vite strips types rather than checking them, so `main.ts` shipped for months with an identifier that resolved to nothing — a failure message reaching for a variable that had gone out of scope, which would have thrown a `ReferenceError` on the one path it was written for. A type checker reads the whole file whether or not a path runs.

The `build` script bundles the tangled source files committed to the repository. Tangling the corpus is a separate step, performed with the published `loom` CLI rather than the in-progress source — using an unverified tangler to tangle the corpus that produces it would be circular. The `prepack` script re-bundles automatically before each publish. The `bun publish` command rewrites the `workspace:*` specifier for `@athrio/loom-lang` — Bun's protocol for local package references — into the real published version number.

{{ version = ${version} }}
{{ effect = ${effect} }}

=>

{
  "name": "@athrio/loom",
  "version": "::[version]",
  "description": "Tangle Loom literate sources into real source files.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "type": "module",
  "bin": {
    "loom": "dist/main.js"
  },
  "files": [
    "dist"
  ],
  "engines": {
    "node": ">=20",
    "bun": ">=1.2"
  },
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "git+https://github.com/athrio-com/loom.git",
    "directory": "packages/loom"
  },
  "publishConfig": {
    "access": "public"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "vite build",
    "typecheck": "tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit",
    "test": "bun test",
    "prepack": "bun run build"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@athrio/loom-config": "workspace:*",
    "@athrio/loom-lang": "workspace:*",
    "@athrio/loom-lang-services": "workspace:*",
    "@effect/platform-node": "::[effect]",
    "effect": "::[effect]",
    "vite": "^8.0.12"
  }
}

## The TypeScript config {Tangle}[tsconfig.json]

The `tsconfig.json` extends the workspace-root `tsconfig.base.json` and covers the `src` directory. Vite produces the `dist` bundle, so the configuration file is a development tool only and never ships with the published package.

=>

{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "out",
    "rootDir": "src"
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}

## The licence {Tangle}[LICENSE]

npm ships a package's `LICENSE` whatever the `files` field says, and until now this package shipped none — an installer had the SPDX expression in the manifest and no text to read. The file below is that text.

It carries the MIT licence in full. Loom was dual-licensed under MIT and Apache-2.0 for a while, inheriting a Rust convention rather than a decision; the Apache branch bought a patent grant nobody was obliged to take, and dropping it left one licence to read and one string to keep consistent.

It also says what is *not* here. Athrio sells Loom Design under a licence of its own, and a reader who finds a permissive text here should not conclude it reaches that product too.

=>

